 /*
$diary has a "page" for each day and each page has a number of entries with time, text, opinion/comment, importance and effects
so you access entry text with $diary[daynumber][entrynumber][$log.text]
.time is simply $time
.text is the diary entry that describes what exactly happened in 1st person
.importance allows filtering out simply moving around and such and is used to decide how to color the text
.comment is a string the player can input, set $diaryprompts to 1 or 2 to be asked for comment whenever an event is done
.opinion (could be about) how you feel about an event and how you've chosen to rationalize it or just the default comment
$log.anythingelse lists some stat changes like money since the last time log widget was used.

to make this work time.twee has a thing that pushes a new day/page into existence at the start of each day, it also reports the date and stat changes of the day
other than that and this there are some widgets at the bottom of widgets.twee and initStory and Start2() from init.twee are used to initialize everything.
And finally nearly all events would ideally have the following bit near the end:

<<set $logprep to "">>
<<log $logprep 3>>
<<logopinion "">>

1st "" being filled with diary entry and 3 beig the importance of event
logopinion could have various opinions about the event split into different $args[] with $diaryprompts 2 or 3 events ask you to pick between them whenever they end

for more complex events usingg $logprep to change entry text based on choices made earlier is useful:

<<set $logprep to "">>
<<set $logprep to $logprep+"">>
<<set $logprep to ""+$logprep>>

<<log $logprep 3>>
<<logopinion "">>

note using variable inside the string ie "$logprep blah" will make it update when $logprep changes while using $logprep+"blah" doesn't do that

Also if it's really necessary <<logadd>> and <<logupdateeffect>> can also be used to change the last entry
for example if you want to have logopinion but there's still a choice with all options ending the event and putting you on street or something.


$logextra will cause <<log>> to (sneakily) add another entry after the current one if it's anything other than ""


<<awarenesswords "some person">>  can be used to set variables like $aw_rapesx to be something like "rapes some person"
<<endcombat>> will do <<awarenesswords "me">> setting $aw_xx to awareness based variation of "rape me", "molest me" or "have sex with me" based on $rapeavoid and $sexavoid
ie.:
<<pronouns>>
<<endcombat>>
<<set $logprep to $He+" "+$aw_xedx"+".">>
<<log $logprep 3>>
<<logopinion "">>

Can have results like "He raped me." and "She touched me weirdly."
<<pronouns>> sets various variables like $he $him $his to match $pronoun since widgets like <<he>> don't work when building a string.


How to Add new variables to be tracked by diary:
1. add it to <<widget "loginit">>'s  <<set log to {...}>> part(so that $log.yourvariable will get it's position in a relevant array)
  update <<set _len to >> to have the last $log. number
  optional: if it's a string make it have "" instead of 0 in $logblank
    <<set $logblank[$log.yourvariable] to ("")>>
2. make <<widget "log">> track changes to it(if it's a number with wide range) or just it's value in some way
  ,$yourvariable - $logged[$log.yourvariable]
  <<set $diary[$days][$diaryline][$log.yourvariable] to ($yourvariable - $logged[$log.yourvariable])>>
3. add it to <<widget "logstatsupdate">> just as ,$yourvariable
4. add it at least to the debug section of <<widget "showdiaryentry">>
  <<if _e[$log.yourvariable] isnot 0 and _e[$log.yourvariable] isnot undefined>>yourvariable:<<print _e[$log.yourvariable]>><</if>>




*/

:: Simple Diary[nobr]
<<back>>
<<for _i, _day range $diary>>
  <br>
  <<for _j, _entry range $diary[_i]>>
    _entry[$log.text]
    _entry[$log.comment]
    <br>
  <</for>>
<</for>>

:: Write Diary

You sit down and start writing to your diary.

<<if $diary.length-1 gt $writtendiary.length>>
It's been  days since your last entry it takes you a moment to recall everything that happened.
<</if>>

[[Next|Write Diary]]
<<link [[Done]]>><<return>><</link>>

:: Diary [nobr]
<<if $canwrite is 1>><<return>><<else>><<set $menu to 2>><<back>> | <<link [[Show all days|Diary]]>><<set $diarymode to 0>><</link>> | <<link [[Show current day|Diary]]>><<set $diarymode to 1>><<set $diarypage to $days>><</link>><</if>>
<<if $canwrite is 1>>
 | <<link [[Show all days|Diary]]>><<set $diarymode to 0>><</link>> filter: <<link [["<"|Diary]]>><<set $diaryfilterB-->><</link>> $diaryfilterB <<link [[">"|Diary]]>><<set $diaryfilterB++>><</link>> 
 | <<set $menu to 1>> <<link [[Show 1 day|Diary]]>><<set $diarymode to 1>><</link>> filter: <<link [["<"|Diary]]>><<set $diaryfilterA-->><</link>> $diaryfilterA <<link [[">"|Diary]]>><<set $diaryfilterA++>><</link>>
 | <<if $logshowcomments is 1>><<link [[Hide|Diary]]>><<set $logshowcomments to 0>><</link>><<else>><<link [[Show|Diary]]>><<set $logshowcomments to 1>><</link>><</if>> comments
<<if $debug is 1>> | <<if $logshowopinions is 1>><<link [[Hide|Diary]]>><<set $logshowopinions to 0>><</link>><<else>><<link [[Show|Diary]]>><<set $logshowopinions to 1>><</link>><</if>> opinions<</if>>
<br>
/*
lists and makes selectable all days
capture _i prevents all of the links setting $diarypage to the last value _i was
could make a year selector since this will probably get annoyingly long after a year or two.
yearlog would probably be an array and use .push($diary) at the end of year before emptying $diary.
compressing or exporting old entries could be a thing
*/
<<if $diarymode is 0>>
  Day:
  <<for _i to 0; _i lte $days; _i++>>
  <<capture _i>>
  <<link [[_i|Diary]]>><<set $diarypage to _i>> <</link>>
  <</capture>>
  <</for>>
<</if>>

<</if>><br>

<<if $canwrite is 1>>
/* originally I was thinking players could add entries on their own but that didn't seem as useful as just adding comments to existing ones */
Change entry $diaryline of day $diarypage's <<link [[comment|Diary]]>><<logcomment $diarypage $diaryline $entry>><</link>><<textbox "$entry" "" >>
or importance(from <<print $diary[$diarypage][$diaryline].importance>> to /* Should I somehow protect entries with above 5 importance? Someone might change them and regret not being able to restore them */
<<link [[0|Diary]]>><<logimportance $diarypage $diaryline 0>><</link>>
<<link [[1|Diary]]>><<logimportance $diarypage $diaryline 1>><</link>>
<<link [[2|Diary]]>><<logimportance $diarypage $diaryline 2>><</link>>
<<link [[3|Diary]]>><<logimportance $diarypage $diaryline 3>><</link>>
<<link [[4|Diary]]>><<logimportance $diarypage $diaryline 4>><</link>>
<<link [[5|Diary]]>><<logimportance $diarypage $diaryline 5>><</link>>)
<<else>>
You aren't in a good spot(such as your bedroom) to edit your diary.
<</if>>


<<if $diarymode is 1>> /* single day mode */
  <br>
  <<for _i, _entry range $diary[$diarypage]>>
  <<capture _i>>
  <<if _entry[$log.importance] gte $diaryfilterA >><br>
    <<if $canwrite is 1>><<link [[■|Diary]]>><<set $diaryline to _i>><</link>><</if>>
    <<if _i gt 1>><<print Math.floor($diary[$diarypage][_i][$log.time] / 60).toString().padStart(2, "0")>>:<<print ($diary[$diarypage][_i][$log.time] % 60).toString().padStart(2, "0")>><</if>>
    <<showdiaryentry $diarypage _i>>
  <</if>>
  <</capture>>
  <</for>>
<<else>> /* all days mode */
  <<run $diary.reverse()>>/* show current day first */
  <<for _i, _day range $diary>>
    <br> /*  */
    <<for _j, _entry range $diary[_i]>>
    <<capture _i, _j>>
    <<if _entry[$log.importance] gte $diaryfilterB >><br>
      <<if $canwrite is 1>><<link [[■|Diary]]>><<set $diarypage to $days-_i>><<set $diaryline to _j>><</link>><</if>> /* $days-_i prevents reversed day order from messing things up */
      <<if _j gt 1>><<print Math.floor($diary[_i][_j][$log.time] / 60).toString().padStart(2, "0")>>:<<print ($diary[_i][_j][$log.time] % 60).toString().padStart(2, "0")>><</if>>
      <<showdiaryentry _i _j>>
    <</if>>
    <</capture>>
    <</for>>
  <</for>>
  <<run $diary.reverse()>>
<</if>>
<br><br>


